+block

–block




The idea that every SCP slot  has to be occupied by an SCP file  exclusively, though, is creatively stifling
附加记录：    

An SCP article is, by definition in and of itself, an SCP article. It exists in-universe. That is the point of the format. That means it either conforms to the formatting standards expected by the Foundation or if it doesn't there's a reason for it. Changing these by-the-definition parameters would be changing the entire basis of why SCPs are separate from tales: canonically, structurally significant differences.

A format screw is a challenge. It's 'how do I make this nontraditional and have the logic check out despite the limitations posed by the fact that this is a formal report document?' Articles like this don't make sense because there is no provided reason for there to be prose in this document.

There's a place for traditional narrative prose on this site, and it's tales. (They're underappreciated; putting their content in SCP slots is not going to address that.)




Edit: The gist of it is what Hippo said below: The format exists for a reason. It should only be broken with care and cunning.





